---
import BaseLayout from '../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import PageHeader from '../../components/sections/PageHeader.astro';
import RelatedContent from '../../components/sections/RelatedContent.astro';
import CTABand from '../../components/sections/CTABand.astro';
import { buildBreadcrumbSchema, buildArticleSchema, buildFAQSchema } from '../../lib/schema';

const title = 'Packaging Design Brief: What to Approve Before Production';
const description = 'Build a production-ready packaging brief covering packed dimensions, dielines, artwork controls, samples, approvals and revision ownership.';
const url = '/blog/packaging-design-services-before-production-2026-08-29';
const datePublished = '2026-08-29';
const dateModified = '2026-10-31';
const image = '/images/blog/custom-mailer-boxes-subscription-brands-banner-v2.webp';

const breadcrumb = buildBreadcrumbSchema([
  { name: 'Home', url: '/' },
  { name: 'Blog', url: '/blog' },
  { name: 'Packaging Design Brief', url }
]);
const article = {
  ...buildArticleSchema({ title, description, url, datePublished, author: 'Apex Packaging Solutions' }),
  dateModified
};
const faq = buildFAQSchema([
  {
    q: 'Is approving a dieline the same as approving the finished package?',
    a: 'No. A dieline defines the flat structural and artwork layout. Buyers still need to approve the packed fit, material and construction specification, artwork proof, closure, labels and any agreed validation plan.'
  },
  {
    q: 'Which packaging file should control a reorder?',
    a: 'Use one revision-controlled approval record that identifies the current dieline, artwork, material and construction specification, approved sample or proof, packing instructions and delivery configuration.'
  },
  {
    q: 'When should a packaging project be reviewed again?',
    a: 'Review the specification when the product, pack count, insert, artwork, closure, packing process, carrier route or distribution environment changes.'
  }
]);
---
<BaseLayout title={title} description={description} image={image} schemaBlocks={[breadcrumb, article, faq]}>
  <PageHeader
    eyebrow="Knowledge base · Design approval"
    heading="Packaging design brief: what to approve before production."
    lead="Turn the chosen packaging concept into one controlled specification for structure, artwork, packout, validation and reorders."
    imageSrc={image}
    imageAlt="Open printed mailer with products and packaging artwork used for a production approval review."
  />

  <section class="py-12 md:py-20">
    <div class="container max-w-default prose-apex">
      <p><strong>A production approval should describe the complete package, not only the artwork.</strong> Buyers need a shared record of the packed dimensions, structure, material callout, print files, closure, labels, packing method and acceptance checks. Without that record, two teams can approve different versions of the same project and find the conflict only after production starts.</p>
      <p>This article begins after the initial concept has been selected. If the structure has not been drawn yet, start with the guide to <a href="/blog/packaging-design-services-send-before-dieline-built">what to send before a dieline is built</a>. For an Apex purchasing enquiry, review <a href="/design-support">packaging design support</a> and send the packed product details, artwork status, quantity and destination.</p>

      <figure class="my-10 overflow-hidden rounded-lg border border-neutral-200 bg-white shadow-card">
        <img src={image} alt="Packaging approval setup with a printed mailer, packed products and artwork references." width="1600" height="720" loading="eager" decoding="async" class="w-full" />
        <figcaption class="px-5 py-4 text-sm text-neutral-600">The approval record should connect the flat drawing to the packed sample and the files released for production.</figcaption>
      </figure>

      <h2>Separate the decisions often called artwork approval</h2>
      <p>A single approval email can hide several different decisions. Assign an owner and a clear status to each one:</p>
      <table>
        <thead><tr><th>Approval</th><th>What it should confirm</th><th>Common gap</th></tr></thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr><td>Structural</td><td>Internal fit, panel dimensions, folds, tabs, glue areas and closure</td><td>The drawing is approved without packing the real contents</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Material</td><td>Named material and construction supplied for the approved design</td><td>Only a colour or generic material family is recorded</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Artwork</td><td>Correct file revision, panel orientation, colour references, copy and legal marks</td><td>An obsolete PDF is released after later edits</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Operational</td><td>Assembly, loading, sealing, labelling, case packing and pallet configuration</td><td>The package works by hand but conflicts with the packing process</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Validation</td><td>Test objective, route assumptions, samples and acceptance criteria</td><td>A test name is copied without defining what a pass means</td></tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>

      <h2>Approve packed dimensions rather than isolated product dimensions</h2>
      <p>Record the product in the condition in which it enters the package. Include caps, sleeves, primary cartons, inner bags, literature, accessories and protective material. State the intended orientation and any surfaces that must not carry load or rub against another component.</p>
      <p>Dimensional tolerance belongs in the conversation too. A cavity that fits one measured sample may not fit the permitted product variation. The packaging designer and product owner should agree which product dimensions are nominal, which can vary and where clearance is needed. That decision cannot be made from length, width and height alone.</p>

      <h2>Lock the dieline before final artwork release</h2>
      <p>The dieline controls cut, score, fold and glue relationships. Artwork should identify the exact dieline revision it uses. Before release, check panel orientation, bleed, safe areas, glue or seal zones, openings, perforations and the location of variable information.</p>
      <p>Barcode artwork needs its own review. GS1 Canada advises keeping a barcode away from package edges, preserving the quiet zone and using a smooth printed surface; placement rules vary for bags, cases and unusual formats. Verify the intended scanning environment instead of treating a visually correct barcode as a scan approval.</p>

      <h2>Use the physical sample to review work, not just appearance</h2>
      <p>A packed sample can reveal issues that a flat proof cannot: slow assembly, product movement, difficult removal, spring-back at the closure, label wrinkling or an opening sequence that exposes the wrong item first. Record the components and revisions used in the sample so the team knows what was actually approved.</p>
      <p><strong>Hypothetical review:</strong> a printed mailer looks correct in the digital proof, but the shipping label crosses a fold after the products are loaded. The artwork itself is not wrong; the operational layout is. Moving the label zone before production is a design-control correction, not a cosmetic preference.</p>

      <h2>Match validation to the real distribution system</h2>
      <p>ISTA states that test selection should begin with familiarity with the actual distribution environment. ASTM’s packaging committee lists separate methods for compression, drop, vibration, conditioning and broader distribution testing. These resources do not make one test suitable for every package; the product, package, route and acceptance criteria still need to be defined.</p>
      <p>Write down what the team is trying to learn. A fit check, print proof, short packing trial and distribution test answer different questions. If a product or package component changes later, use the change record to decide which checks need to be repeated.</p>

      <h2>Create one production-release checklist</h2>
      <ol>
        <li>Identify the product, pack count, orientation, packed weight and dimensional tolerances.</li>
        <li>Record the final dieline number and the matching artwork filename or revision.</li>
        <li>Record the proposed material and complete construction description from the quotation.</li>
        <li>Confirm closure, sealing, label and barcode locations on an assembled pack.</li>
        <li>Approve print content, variable-data areas and required product or regulatory copy.</li>
        <li>Document the packing sequence, outer case and pallet configuration where relevant.</li>
        <li>Define sample, proof and validation acceptance criteria before testing.</li>
        <li>Name the people authorized to approve structure, artwork and commercial release.</li>
        <li>Archive the approved files and record what changes require reapproval.</li>
      </ol>

      <h2>Frequently asked questions</h2>
      <h3>Is approving a dieline the same as approving the finished package?</h3>
      <p>No. The dieline defines the flat layout. The team still needs to approve packed fit, the material and construction specification, artwork proof, closure, labels and any validation plan.</p>
      <h3>Which file should control a reorder?</h3>
      <p>Use one revision-controlled record that identifies the current dieline, artwork, construction, approved sample or proof, packing instructions and delivery configuration.</p>
      <h3>When should the package be reviewed again?</h3>
      <p>Review it when the product, pack count, insert, artwork, closure, packing process, carrier route or distribution environment changes.</p>

      <h2>Primary references</h2>
      <ul>
        <li><a href="https://www.gs1ca.org/barcodes/" rel="noopener noreferrer">GS1 Canada: barcode placement, quiet zones and verification</a></li>
        <li><a href="https://www.ista.org/getting_started_with_design.php" rel="noopener noreferrer">ISTA: getting started with package design and testing</a></li>
        <li><a href="https://mcsdocs.astm.org/committee-documents/D10-Fact-Sheet-2025.pdf" rel="noopener noreferrer">ASTM Committee D10 fact sheet: packaging test methods</a></li>
      </ul>
    </div>
  </section>

  <RelatedContent items={[
    { title: 'Packaging Design Support', href: '/design-support', description: 'Review the commercial design-support page for dielines, artwork and project enquiries.' },
    { title: 'Before a Dieline Is Built', href: '/blog/packaging-design-services-send-before-dieline-built', description: 'Prepare the product and process inputs needed before structural design begins.' },
    { title: 'Packaging RFQ Template', href: '/blog/custom-packaging-rfq-template-12-specs-buyers-include', description: 'Organize the 12 specification fields needed for a comparable quote.' }
  ]} />

  <CTABand
    heading="Release one controlled packaging specification."
    lead="Send the packed product details, artwork status, quantity and destination when you are ready to discuss design support or production pricing."
    primaryCta={{ label: 'Discuss a packaging project', href: '/contact' }}
  />
</BaseLayout>
